libbtbb (2020-12-R1+fork5) unstable; urgency=low
  * A capture file in pcapng format is created in a few milliseconds rather
    than a few hundred. The file reserves a page for its section header block
    and another for its interface description, and it filled both by writing
    one zero byte per system call: about 65,000 of them on a host with 16 kB
    pages, which took 300 ms on an Apple laptop where the same file now takes
    7 ms. The bytes written are unchanged.
  * The library is installed to the directory the system keeps libraries in.
    It went to lib under the prefix unless a build command asked for
    somewhere else with LIB_SUFFIX, so a 64-bit build on most Linux systems
    other than Debian and Ubuntu put it in lib while everything else on the
    machine looks in lib64, and a Debian or Ubuntu build into /usr put it in
    /usr/lib rather than the multiarch /usr/lib/<triplet>; a program then
    linked it only where the compiler's own search path covered for it. The
    library's directory and the header's now come from GNUInstallDirs, and
    CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR and CMAKE_INSTALL_INCLUDEDIR choose them. LIB_SUFFIX
    still decides where it is set to something, so a build command that
    passes it installs where it always did, and the configure step says which
    of the two it followed. libbtbb.pc names the directories used. The
    cleanup script looks in lib64 and in a multiarch directory as well as in
    lib, and takes the pkg-config file away with the library it names; it
    also finds a static archive and a macOS .dylib, where it used to look for
    a .so and nothing else, and knows about btbb.h, the header this library
    installs, which it did not name.
  * The packaging files are gone. The RPM spec, the MacPorts Portfile and the
    Homebrew formula in packaging/ described earlier releases: the spec listed
    libbtbb.so.0 and two headers where the build produces libbtbb.so.1 and
    installs one, and the Portfile and the formula fetched the 2014-02-R2
    source. Distributions, MacPorts and Homebrew each keep their own packaging
    next to the version they ship, so the files are removed rather than kept in
    step with every release, and nothing in the tree describes a library the
    build does not produce. README has a "Packaging" section pointing at the
    build and install instructions.
  * The record of the packet that settles a piconet's address part carries
    that packet's own header again. Dewhitening a header takes the piconet's
    CLK1-6 rather than the clock the receiving board read the packet at, and
    the offset between the two is what the address-part search recovers
    alongside the address part. The library had stopped reporting that offset
    until the whole CLK1-27 was known, so a capture tool asking for it got 0
    and read the header at its own board's clock: the same symbols dewhitened
    to 18 bits of a header nobody sent, whose check bits did not come out and
    whose type field read as a packet that was never sent. The record of the
    one packet a reader most needs - the first evidence the address part was
    derived from - carried those bits. btbb_piconet_get_clk_offset() now
    reports the offset from the moment CLK1-6 is known, in the 625 us slots
    that clock counts, and btbb.h says which clock, and so which unit, each
    stage of recovery reports. The library's own hop arithmetic keeps working
    from the separate slot field it has used since the two were told apart,
    so nothing internal reads one unit for the other.
  * A survey piconet can be read while the survey is still running. The only
    way into a survey's results was btbb_next_survey_result(), which takes
    each piconet out of the survey, so a tool could not read the UAP the
    survey had recovered for a LAP without giving up the survey's own copy
    of it - which is why ubertooth-rx -z, whose job is finding addresses,
    wrote capture records with no reference address in them. The new
    btbb_survey_piconet(lap) hands back the live piconet for a LAP, or NULL
    where the survey has not heard that LAP, and leaves it in the survey to
    go on being updated. Taking a piconet out with btbb_next_survey_result()
    now removes its entry from the survey's hash table as well as from the
    front of its list: the entry used to be freed while the table's buckets
    still pointed at it, so a survey carrying on after some of its results
    had been read looked that LAP up again for every further packet of that
    piconet and wrote through a piconet that had been released; the table
    and its buckets were also never freed, so each survey that was read to
    the end left them behind. Both allocations the survey's own code makes
    for a LAP it has just heard - its entry for that LAP, and the piconet -
    are checked, and the packet is dropped where either fails; whichever had
    failed used to be written through and the tool died with it. Either
    failure is reported on standard error, as btbb_packet_new() reports a
    packet it could not allocate, and btbb_piconet_new() returns NULL where
    it used to write through its own failed allocation. Library version 1.2,
    for the new function.
  * A clock the library gave up is not chosen again. Discovery starting over
    had no memory of the clock that had just failed, so a piconet could lock
    the same wrong clock, decode nothing, give it up, and go round that loop
    for as long as the capture ran. Each piconet now remembers the clocks it
    has given up for its LAP, cleared when the LAP changes, and the search
    skips them, so each attempt rules one more out. Two cases are left alone:
    a clock that did decode a header before it stopped working is given up
    but not ruled out, because it may be the right clock on a link that went
    bad, and a rejection is only held against a search using the same UAP,
    because a failed header says the clock or the UAP was wrong without
    saying which.
  * A caller can hand a clock back. The library gives a clock up on headers
    that fail their HEC, which is evidence about CLK1-6 and the UAP. A wrong
    CLK1-27 fails differently: it carries the CLK1-6 discovery confirmed, so
    it dewhitens and checks out every header it receives and gets only the
    channel wrong, and its symptom is silence rather than failure. The
    library, called per packet with no timer of its own, cannot judge that,
    so btbb_piconet_drop_clock() lets a capture tool that knows how long it
    has been following say so. The clock is ruled out and discovery starts
    again, exactly as after a clock the library gave up, so the next attempt
    does not return to it. Over a sweep of 400 clock values, the 22 wrong
    clocks the library accepted and kept heard 65 of the next 1000 slot pairs
    on average and 142 at most, where every clock that was right heard all
    1000, and all 22 are handed back and ruled out. Additive: existing
    callers are unaffected.
  * A clock whose headers all fail is given up on half as many of them once
    the failures have been going for two seconds. Twenty failed headers
    arrive in 24 ms on a link carrying traffic, but at four packets a second
    they take 4.75 s and on a slower link longer still, and all that time the
    receiver hops on an offset that has never decoded anything. Ten are now
    enough there, which is 2.25 s at that rate. It cannot be quicker than the
    packets arrive: a clock that hears nothing at all leaves nothing to
    count, and the library is called per packet and keeps no timer.
  * A piconet that nothing works for is reported once rather than on every
    loss. After three clocks have been given up for one piconet - which is
    what a piconet that has ended or left range looks like - the library says
    so and then keeps looking quietly, instead of writing a note for every
    loss for as long as the capture runs. A clock that decodes a header
    starts that count again, so a loss after a lock that worked is reported.
  * btaptap decodes keystrokes again. It read every byte of a capture through
    ord(), which python 3 raises a TypeError for, and the read loop treated
    any TypeError as the end of the capture, so the tool printed nothing and
    exited 0 on every capture it was given, and the -e Ellisys path refused
    its own example export because text mode had turned the line endings it
    compares against into something else. The three parsers now index the
    bytes they are given, the Ellisys export is read as python 3 reads a file
    and its hex converted with bytes.fromhex(), and the loop ends on what the
    reader returns at the end of a capture. The recorded Ellisys export in
    the repository, 864 keyboard reports, now gives up the paragraph that
    was typed into it. Anything that stops the
    capture being read through - a file that is not a capture, a record header
    that does not describe a packet, a data link layer btaptap does not read -
    is now one "error:" line on standard error and a non-zero exit, where the
    tool used to exit 0 or stop with a traceback. The pcapdump module the
    README offers writes a capture file again, where joining the bytes of its
    header raised a TypeError, and a record's timestamp is worked out from the
    fraction of a second it holds rather than written after the decimal point:
    a fraction that did not fill its field came out up to a second late, 5
    microseconds past the second reading as half a second. Which unit that
    fraction is in is taken from the capture's magic number, so the
    nanosecond-resolution files this library's own writer produces - the
    Ubertooth tools' pcap files - are read as nanoseconds, and a file that is
    not a capture at all is refused rather than read as one, which the magic
    number check could not do before. A capture a tool was killed in the
    middle of writing is reported too, where part of a record read as the end
    of the capture. A capture taken at a snapshot length is read rather than
    called corrupt - a packet longer than that length is what the field it
    tripped over is for - and the module's writer puts a record's two lengths
    in the order the format has them, where a capture holding the first bytes
    of longer packets had them the wrong way round.
  * btaptap's command line is checked. An unknown option, a missing option
    value, a count that is not a number, an option given a value twice, -r
    together with -e, and -c without -r were all accepted in silence, some of
    them doing nothing; each is now one "error:" line and exit 1. -h prints
    the usage text on standard output rather than standard error, and beside a
    capture it no longer cancels it: the keystrokes come first and the usage
    text last.
  * A build asking for no library is refused while configuring. The check for
    it named an option that does not exist, so "cmake -DBUILD_SHARED_LIB=OFF"
    - the obvious way to ask for a static build, which also has to set
    BUILD_STATIC_LIB=ON - configured a tree with no library in it and failed
    at the link of the first test program with "library 'btbb-static' not
    found". The refusal now names both options, and the test programs link
    whichever library the configuration built.
  * A Tx Power Level in advertising data is printed as the signed value the
    specification defines, so lell_print() reports -12 dBm rather than
    244 dBm. It was read through a plain char, which is unsigned on every ARM
    host - a Raspberry Pi among them - and signed on the x86 and Apple ones,
    so the same capture printed differently depending on where it was read.
  * A capture file's interface description is no longer read past the end of
    the option list that carries it. The description was handed to the pcapng
    writer as a list of options with no terminating option, so a description
    of 253 characters or more left both the writer and the timestamp
    resolution check reading an option header from the bytes after the list,
    and writing whatever they found there into the file. Ubertooth's tools
    pass a nine-character description, so their captures were never affected.
  * An allocation the library makes and cannot get is reported rather than
    written through. The FEC 2/3 decoder and tun_format() return NULL, as
    btbb_packet_new() already did. btbb_init() returns -1 when it cannot
    build the whole access code error correction table, having thrown away
    the part it did build: a table with a hole in it stops putting right the
    error patterns the missing entries describe, and nothing used to say so.
    The hash table that table lives in allocates on its own account as well,
    and that allocation still ends the process, which the entry below covers.
  * An allocation failure inside a hash table says why the process ended. The
    library keeps two tables: the access code error correction table
    btbb_init() builds, and the piconet survey's table of the LAPs it has
    heard. Each is grown inside macros that expand in the caller and have no
    error path to return, so a failed allocation there ends the process rather
    than work through a table that is not there. It now prints "error:
    libbtbb: out of memory" on standard error and exits with status 1, the
    status every other failure uses, where it used to print nothing and exit
    255. btbb.h records this for both. The entries themselves are allocated by
    the library and checked as before: btbb_init() returns -1, and the survey
    drops the packet.
  * An advertising PDU's length is read from the whole header octet, as the
    Bluetooth Core specification has defined it since 5.0. Six bits were read,
    which was the 4.x header, so an extended advertising PDU on a primary
    channel - past 63 octets, where a legacy one stops at 37 - was reported
    as its length modulo 64: a 64-octet PDU came out as 0 bytes, and both
    lell_print() and the capture files held none of its payload. The whole
    range of the field now fits too: the symbol buffer is 264 bytes, which is
    the access address, the header, the 255 octets the field can claim, and
    the CRC. It was 260, four short, so a PDU whose Length was 252 to 255 lost
    its last octets, and three of them were printed and recorded as its CRC
    while the real CRC was gone. Extended advertising reaches those lengths
    through AUX_ADV_IND and AUX_CHAIN_IND, and so does a data PDU at its full
    payload on an encrypted link, whose Length counts the MIC as well. LE
    capture files declare a snapshot length four bytes longer to match, 274
    for the plain writer and 288 for the PPI one.
  * An LE capture file's reference access address flag now follows the
    address the caller gave rather than the channel the packet arrived on.
    The published layout defines that field as the access address configured
    into the capture tool, so a tool following a connection earns the flag on
    a data channel, where it used to be cleared, and a caller that gave no
    address does not earn it on an advertising channel, where it used to be
    set. Ubertooth passes the advertising address on an advertising channel
    and none on a data channel, so its own capture files are unchanged.
  * A clock and UAP the library recovered that turn out not to be the
    piconet's are now given up. Following a piconet never winnowed again, so a
    wrong CLK1-6 or UAP meant every packet printed "Failed to decode packet"
    for as long as the capture ran, with no way back. After 20 packet headers
    in a row fail their HEC - which is checked against the piconet's UAP over
    a header dewhitened with CLK1-6, so it is evidence about both, where a
    payload that will not decode is only a packet lost to bit errors -
    btbb_process_packet() reports BTBB_PROCESS_LOCK_LOST and the piconet
    starts discovery again. A clock the caller set itself, as ubertooth-follow
    does from an adapter, is left alone.
  * A long LE pcapng capture now says when a CONNECT_IND's connection
    parameters were not recorded. Room for them is set aside in the interface
    description block when the file is created, about 340 connections' worth
    on a 16 kB page, and a capture that filled it used to keep writing packets
    and quietly stop recording the parameters needed to follow the
    connections in them. lell_pcapng_append_packet() now returns
    LELL_PCAPNG_CONNECT_INFO_NOT_RECORDED, a positive value where its errors
    are negative, so a capture tool can warn once.
  * Decoding a packet no longer prints it. btbb_decode() wrote "Packet decoded
    with clock 0x.. (rv=..)", which carries a number meaningful only inside the
    library, and a dump of the packet, both to stdout, whenever a packet
    decoded. A tool following a piconet had every packet printed twice, once
    there and once where it asked for it; any other caller got a dump it had
    not asked for at all. btbb_print_packet() prints a packet as it always
    has.
  * Progress and complaints from UAP and clock discovery go to stderr. About
    twenty lines of running commentary ("1234 initial CLK1-27 candidates",
    "Acquired CLK1-27 = 0x1234567", "failed to confirm UAP" and the rest) used
    to go to stdout, so a tool dumping its capture there had them mixed into
    the dump with nothing to tell them from capture data. The AFH map a caller
    asks for with btbb_print_afh_map() is still printed on stdout.
  * btbb_find_ac_n() searches a symbol stream whose length it is told, and
    reads no further whatever search length it is given. btbb_find_ac(), whose
    signature carries no length to check, is unchanged: it reads the
    search_length + 63 symbols it always has.
  * Asking for aliased hop reversal, where a receiver observes the 79 channels
    folded into 25, is refused with a note on stderr. It used to be accepted
    and then reversed as if unaliased, choosing clock candidates against
    channels such a receiver never reports.
  * The hopping sequence notes ("Calculating complete hopping sequence.",
    "Hopping sequence calculated." and "Found hopping sequence in cache.") are
    gone, along with the sequence they announced. A tool streaming its capture
    to stdout no longer has them mixed into the stream.
  * The LE decoder now bounds the channel a frequency outside 2402-2480 MHz
    decodes as. A frequency below the band used to come back as a channel
    index of 254 or 255, which is no channel at all, and 2400 MHz as a PCAP
    record saying 2912 MHz.
  * Recovering a piconet's clock no longer writes about 200 kB past the end of
    its candidate list when adaptive frequency hopping (AFH) is in use. The
    list was sized as though the hopping sequence visited all 79 channels
    evenly, which it does not under AFH, and nothing stopped the search
    filling it past the end. Any map with 20 to 39 used channels overflowed,
    once the library had decided the piconet was hopping adaptively - which
    the first-packet bug below could also make it decide wrongly.
  * The first packet of a clock recovery attempt no longer makes the library
    announce "Hopping pattern appears to be AFH" and adapt to a map it never
    observed. It compared that packet with a non-existent previous one, so
    roughly one attempt in 79 - whenever the first packet arrived on 2402 MHz -
    started out searching the wrong hopping sequence and could not find the
    clock.
  * Hop predictions during clock recovery now follow the AFH map as it is
    learned. They used to come from a hopping sequence generated once, when
    recovery started, so every channel seen for the first time afterwards left
    the predictions describing a map the library no longer held, and the real
    clock was discarded along with the wrong candidates. Recovery against an
    adaptive piconet can still restart while the map is incomplete, since the
    predictions can only be as good as the map learned so far, but it no longer
    throws the clock away over a map it already has. It also predicts hops one
    at a time instead of building a 128 MB table of the whole sequence first,
    so it starts without that allocation or the wait for it.
  * The hop pattern remembered while reversing hops is bounded, as it already
    was while finding the UAP, rather than running off the end of the array
    after 1000 packets.
  * A piconet released while its clock was still being recovered no longer
    leaks its candidate list.
  * The 625 us slot offset found with the address part and the 312.5 us CLKN
    tick offset found with CLK1-27 are separate fields inside the library, so
    its own hop arithmetic can no longer read one for the other: one field
    used to carry both, written in one unit and read in the other. btbb.h says
    which of the two btbb_piconet_get_clk_offset() reports at each stage of
    recovery.
  * AUX1 payloads decode. The payload header's length was being thrown away
    for this packet type, so every AUX1 packet reported a payload of 0 bytes.
  * EV3, EV4 and EV5 payloads decode. The eSCO types carry no length in the
    packet, and the search for it re-read the first byte of the payload for
    every byte of it (EV3 and EV5) or checked the CRC at a length the payload
    cannot have, reading before the start of the payload (EV4). None of the
    three could decode a payload at the longest length its type allows either.
  * An eSCO payload is reported at the length the caller says the link was set
    up with, and otherwise at the longest length whose CRC agrees - except
    within a run of lengths that agree only because the bytes between them are
    zero, where it takes the second length of the run, since nothing in the
    packet says whether the payload ends at the first or at the second. The
    search used to stop at the first length that agreed. One payload in 256 ends its CRC in a zero byte, which makes the
    length one byte short of it agree as well, so the search stopped there and
    dropped the payload's last information byte; shorter lengths also agree by
    chance. A payload at the longest length its type carries, which is what a
    link normally sets up, now comes out right: over 20,000 random payloads
    per type, EV3 reported 79 wrong lengths before this and 1 after, EV5 132
    and 0, EV4 117 and 0. A payload shorter than that can now come out one byte
    too long instead of one byte too short, since a zero byte of noise right
    after it agrees as well; a caller following a known link should say its
    length with btbb_packet_set_esco_length(), which skips the guess and is
    the only way to get the length right on an encrypted link, where no CRC
    ever agrees.
  * An HV3 voice packet is no longer reported as 32 bytes of EV3. The two
    share a packet type code, and the library chose between them by decoding
    as EV3 and falling back to HV3 only when the capture ran out before the
    search had finished, which a real capture does not. An HV3 carries no CRC
    for the search to agree with, so no length agreed and the packet was
    reported at the longest one anyway: its 30 bytes of voice and two bytes
    that were not part of the packet. Only a CRC that agrees now says EV3.
  * A DV packet's data field decodes. It was being dewhitened with the 80 bits
    of whitening that belong to the packet's voice field, so the payload
    header's length, the data and the CRC were all wrong.
  * A payload shorter than the CRC it would carry is no longer reported as
    having passed a CRC check. There is nothing to check at that length, and
    the check was reading the CRC from before the payload.
  * A packet cut short by the end of a capture no longer decodes a payload, or
    a packet header, out of symbols the capture did not hold.
  * A packet whose payload was cut short, or lost to noise, now reports the
    bytes that were decoded, or none at all, instead of the length its payload
    header claimed.
  * The access code search no longer reads part of a sync word when it is
    given no offsets to search. The length its caller has to provide is now
    documented as the one it actually reads, 63 symbols past the search
    window rather than 72.
  * The connection parameters a PCAPNG file records for a CONNECT_IND are now
    the ones the packet carried. They were taken six bytes early, so the
    access address was the first four bytes of the advertiser's address, the
    hop increment came from the supervision timeout, and the last six bytes
    were not recorded at all, leaving a reader unable to follow the
    connection.
  * BR/EDR records now say whether the header's HEC and the payload's CRC were
    checked and whether they held, in both PCAP and PCAPNG files. Every packet
    read as "neither checked" before, so nothing downstream could tell a
    verified payload from a guess. A voice payload, which carries no CRC, is
    recorded as having had none checked, and so is a packet recorded while the
    library is still guessing the piconet's upper address part, where a CRC
    failure says the guess was wrong rather than anything about the packet.
    A record only reports a result when the reference address part it carries
    is the one the result was reached with, which is what the format's reader
    has to go on.
  * A failed or incomplete write to a PCAPNG file is reported to the caller
    instead of returning success, and the part of a record that did reach the
    file is taken back out, so a capture that runs out of disk space is
    noticed and still ends on a whole record a reader can parse.
  * A BR/EDR PCAPNG record no longer carries up to three bytes of whatever the
    caller's memory held in the padding that rounds the record to a 4-byte
    boundary.
  * The interface options a PCAPNG file records for a CONNECT_IND, a BD_ADDR
    and a clock now carry the length of their contents, four bytes shorter
    than before, so a reader walking the options stays in step with them.
  * The spare room in a PCAPNG header block is filled with as many padding
    options as it takes. On a host with 64 kB memory pages one option could
    not cover it and the length written was truncated, leaving the block
    malformed.
  * A capture that records more interface options than the space set aside for
    them now refuses the extra ones instead of writing past the end of the
    header block.
  * The snapshot length a capture file declares is the longest record its
    writer can produce. A BR/EDR file said 400 bytes where a record reaches
    422, so a reader sizing a buffer from it had too little room, and an LE
    file said 400 where 274 is the longest record it writes, and a PPI file
    the same where 288 is.
  * Writing a record for a packet with no payload, and recording a reference
    UAP of 0x80 or above, no longer rely on behaviour C leaves undefined.
  * A record carries only a payload the decoder produced for that packet.
    While the library is still guessing a piconet's upper address part it
    decodes the same packet at candidate clocks and throws the results away,
    and the last one it tried used to end up in the capture.
  * btbb_pcapng_close() and lell_pcapng_close() return 0 on success. They
    reported a failure on every close before, and now report one when the
    file could not be closed, as the PCAP writers do.
  * New packet flags BTBB_HEC_CHECKED, BTBB_HEC_VALID and BTBB_CRC_CHECKED,
    which with the existing BTBB_CRC_CORRECT say what the decoder checked and
    what it found. BTBB_CRC_CORRECT was never set before.
  * Reading a piconet's AFH map no longer builds a whole hopping sequence.
    btbb_piconet_set_afh_map() generated it whenever the address was known: a
    128 MB allocation and a wait of seconds, paid every time ubertooth-follow
    read an adapter's map, for a sequence nothing reads any more. Hops are
    answered one at a time from the map instead, so the allocation is gone -
    and with it the failure path of the largest allocation the library made.
  * Clock recovery no longer takes the first candidate clock left standing as
    the piconet's. While the AFH map is still incomplete the predicted hops
    are wrong for the channels not yet seen, so a clock that is not the
    piconet's can match every hop observed so far and be the last one left.
    The library then followed the piconet on that offset, and following never
    winnows again, so every packet after it failed to decode and there was no
    way back. Four further hops now have to agree with a candidate before it
    is accepted, which also gives the map time to gain a channel that rules it
    out. Simulated against a device whose map the observer is one channel
    short of, a wrong clock is accepted about a tenth as often: 10 of 400
    recoveries, where 99 of the same 400 locked one before. It is not
    impossible, and a piconet followed on a wrong clock still has no way back.
    What it is traded for is restarts, which are recoverable: in the same 400,
    the clock went unfound within the hops observed 276 times rather than 194.
    btbb_winnow() also does nothing once the clock is known, rather than
    comparing the hop it was found on again.
  * An LE advertising PDU whose payload is shorter than the fields read from
    it - because it claims more than arrived, or because its length field is
    too small for its own type - no longer has those fields printed out of
    bytes that were never received. A CONNECT_REQ carrying ten bytes reported
    a connection access address, a hop increment and a clock accuracy read
    from whatever followed in the buffer. The dump now says what is missing,
    on stderr, and prints the bytes that did arrive.
  * An LE data PDU that arrived with no payload no longer reports an LL
    Control opcode, which was read from the first byte of its CRC.
  * A flags element with no flags in it no longer has a flags byte read from
    past the end of the advertising data.
  * The UAP is widened before it is shifted into a piconet's address. A signed
    int cannot hold a UAP of 0x80 or more shifted up 24 places, so predicting
    hops for one of those addresses was undefined behaviour, which the
    sanitizers report.
  * A packet whose payload did not decode no longer says it has one.
    BTBB_HAS_PAYLOAD was set on every decode, and it is the flag the capture
    writers and btbb_print_packet() read to decide whether there is a payload
    to report, so a packet the decoder gave up on was reported with the
    length and bytes the last packet decoded into the same object had left
    behind - and btbb_find_ac() reuses one object across packets. The flag
    now says only what was decoded, the payload field is cleared when nothing
    was, and a packet with no payload still has its type and payload header
    printed.
  * BR/EDR records now carry the number of bits the forward error correction
    put right, in both PCAP and PCAPNG files: the bits of the 18-bit packet
    header whose three symbols disagreed, and one bit for each rate 2/3 block
    of the payload whose check symbols did not match its data. Both fields
    read 0 before, whatever the reception had been, and the format gives
    neither a flag that would let a reader tell "none" from "not counted".
  * A PCAP capture that cannot finish writing a record takes it back out, so
    the file ends on a whole record rather than on a record header whose
    length runs past the end of the file. The PCAPNG writer already did this
    with a block. The PCAP writers also write straight through rather than
    through a buffer, since a buffer cannot be discarded without being
    flushed first, and each record was already flushed as it was written.
  * Choosing the CLK1-27 candidates walks the clock values once instead of
    twice. The walk is 2^21 values, each one's hop computed from the AFH map
    as it stands, and it runs inside the packet callback of whatever is
    capturing, every time the map gains a channel that changes the sequence.
    Measured on an Apple silicon Mac, best of seven: 27-30 ms down to 8-11 ms
    for the basic sequence, and 41-48 ms down to 21-25 ms with the 20 used
    channels AFH permits. The candidates are the same ones, and the list is
    grown as they are found, so they stay inside it however many there are.
  * Three symbols the library exported, hop(), gen_hop_pattern() and
    get_hop_pattern(), are gone with the hopping sequence. None of them is
    declared in btbb.h, so no documented caller can be using them, but a
    program linked against an older libbtbb that called them will not find
    them in this one.
  * New TESTING.md: what the test suite covers and where its vectors come from,
    every defect the tests found and what a user saw, and the numbers the fixes
    moved.
  * btaptap now finds the pcapdump module of the install it belongs to. The
    module goes into the install prefix's site directory, which the
    interpreter searches only when that prefix is the one it was built for, so
    an install with a prefix of its own - or a staged package - gave "No
    module named 'pcapdump'" as soon as the script was run. It now looks in
    the prefix it is installed in, as a fallback after the plain import has
    failed.
  * Installing into a second prefix puts the pcapdump module there too.
    Installing it was left to pip with --upgrade, which installs nothing -
    and says so, and reports success - when the same version is already
    installed where the interpreter itself looks, so the second prefix came
    out with btaptap in it and no module.
  * A capture records the payload of the packet that settles a piconet's
    upper address part. That packet is the first evidence the address part
    was derived from, and its record carried a correct-CRC result over a
    payload a reader could not see: the trial clock whose CRC came out is the
    packet's own decode, so the payload it produced now reaches the capture
    file. Records written for a candidate clock the search rejected still
    carry no payload, because those bytes are the trial's rather than the
    packet's, and a clock ruled out by the piconet's own address part - which
    is what a known UAP does to another piconet on the same LAP - is no
    longer decoded at all, so nothing of it reaches a record either.
  * The interface was compared with 2020-12-R1 before the release, by reading
    the debug information of both shared libraries with abidiff. The soname
    stays libbtbb.so.1: a program that uses the library through btbb.h goes
    on working, and a program that declared one of the library's internal
    symbols for itself is the one case that does not, as the entry about
    hop() in this release already describes. btbb.h, the only header the
    install offers, has gained six
    functions - btbb_find_ac_n(), btbb_packet_set_esco_length(),
    btbb_packet_get_esco_length(), btbb_piconet_drop_clock(),
    lell_allocate_and_decode_n() and lell_get_payload_length() - and five
    constants, and has lost nothing; no function it declares has changed its
    signature; and pkt_queue, the one
    structure it defines rather than leaves opaque, is unchanged. The
    structures behind btbb_packet, btbb_piconet and lell_packet have all
    grown, which a program cannot see, because no installed header describes
    them and the library allocates every one of them. The library version is
    1.1, where 2020-12-R1's was 1.0.
  * Behavior an existing caller sees even with that unchanged interface comes
    in three groups, each recorded in its own entry above or in an earlier
    release. Two functions have gained a positive return value where they
    answered 0: btbb_process_packet() answers BTBB_PROCESS_LOCK_LOST when it
    gives up a clock, and lell_pcapng_append_packet() answers
    LELL_PCAPNG_CONNECT_INFO_NOT_RECORDED when the packet reached the file
    but the connection it belongs to could not be recorded. A caller reading
    anything other than 0 as failure sees a failure that is not one; one
    testing for a negative value is unaffected. Next, the capture writers
    report a write that did not reach the file, where
    btbb_pcap_append_packet() and its three siblings answered 0 whether the
    bytes got there or not. Last, a decode says less where it knows less: a
    packet whose payload did not decode no longer reports a payload, an eSCO
    payload is reported at the length its type and CRC settle on rather than
    the first length whose CRC agreed, btbb_init_hop_reversal() refuses the
    aliased case it does not implement instead of answering as though it had
    a sequence for it, and lell_print()'s complaints about a malformed packet
    go to standard error rather than into the packet dump on standard output.
  * btbb_pcap_open() is no longer exported. It carries the library's public
    prefix but no installed header declares it, so nothing following the
    header could call it. A new abi_surface test holds the exported names to
    what btbb.h declares, so a public function cannot go missing unnoticed
    and an internal one cannot join the interface unnoticed. Six more
    internal symbols stopped being exported earlier in this release and are
    named here together for anyone tracking them: btbb_pcap_dump() and
    count_bits(), with the work they belonged to, libbtbb_section_options
    with the capture writer, and hop() with the hopping sequence, as the
    entry above describes, along with gen_hop_pattern() and
    get_hop_pattern().
  * The library exports only the functions btbb.h declares. Thirty-two
    helpers of its own - single_hop(), precalc, pcapng_create(), crc_check()
    and the rest - were exported under plain names, where each reads as part
    of the interface to anyone looking at the symbol table, and becomes a
    name the library cannot change once a program has taken it up. One of
    them, bt_compidtostr(), is also a function in BlueZ's libbluetooth, so a
    program linking both libraries got whichever the link order chose. The
    sources are now compiled with -fvisibility=hidden, and each declaration
    in btbb.h carries a BTBB_API macro, which is what gives that one function
    default visibility again; on Windows the same macro is the dllexport or
    dllimport declaration a shared library needs. A shared libbtbb exports 96
    names where it exported 128, and those 96 are exactly what btbb.h
    declares. The soname stays libbtbb.so.1: nothing btbb.h declares has
    gone, so a program built against the header goes on working. A program
    that had linked one of the 32 by reading it out of the symbol table has
    to be rebuilt against the header.
    The abi_surface test now holds the exported set to the declared one, on
    Linux and on macOS, and the tests that reach internal helpers compile the
    sources they need rather than linking the library.
  * libbtbb.pc names the directories the library and the header are really
    in. A program linked with `pkg-config --libs libbtbb` was given a -L for
    a directory holding no library, and linked by luck where the compiler's
    own search path covered for it, or not at all where it did not. Two
    things were wrong. The libdir and includedir came from GNUInstallDirs,
    which answers lib/x86_64-linux-gnu where the prefix is /usr on a
    multiarch distribution and lib64 on others, while the library is
    installed to lib${LIB_SUFFIX} and the header to include. And the prefix
    was the one given when the build was configured, so installing the same
    build somewhere else - `cmake --install build --prefix somewhere`, which
    is how a package is staged - left a file pointing back at a directory the
    files are not in. The prefix is now worked out from where libbtbb.pc
    itself is, so it follows the files wherever they are installed or moved
    to, and the walk back to it counts the directories LIB_SUFFIX asks for:
    it can carry a path rather than a suffix, and the Debian package builds
    with -DLIB_SUFFIX=/x86_64-linux-gnu. A build whose CMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR
    asks for a directory the install does not use now says so while
    configuring, and names -DLIB_SUFFIX= as the way to choose one.

  * Manufacturer-specific advertising data decodes the company name for
    identifiers assigned since 2020. The table came from BlueZ and ended at
    identifier 1037, so a packet from any company registered after that showed
    "not assigned", and 124 of the names it did carry had since been renamed or
    changed hands. It is regenerated from the Bluetooth SIG's published Assigned
    Numbers: 4133 identifiers named where 1038 were, and every one of the 4060 in
    the SIG's current list by the name the SIG gives it. The 73 identifiers the
    earlier table named that the SIG no longer lists are kept, so a capture of an
    older device still decodes by name.

 -- Russell Wagner <152923636+SqueakyFloors@users.noreply.github.com>  27 Oct 2026 22:30:00 -0700

libbtbb (2020-12-R1+fork4) unstable; urgency=low
  * LE packets from Bluetooth 4.2 and later devices keep their whole payload.
    A data PDU's length is read from all 8 bits of the field and allowed up to
    the 251 bytes Data Length Extension permits, frame storage holds a complete
    260-byte packet, and PCAP and PCAPNG files record the full packet instead
    of the first 64 bytes.
  * Fixed the captured and original lengths in LE PCAP records, which were the
    wrong way round, and stopped the PCAPNG writer copying more than the
    record holds.
  * New decoder entry point lell_allocate_and_decode_n(), which takes the
    number of bytes captured so it cannot read past the end of a caller's
    buffer, and reports a payload no longer than the capture holds. The
    64-byte lell_allocate_and_decode() stays for existing callers.
  * An allocation failure in the LE decoder is no longer dereferenced.
  * An LE PCAPNG record no longer carries up to three bytes of whatever the
    caller's memory held in the padding that rounds the record to a 4-byte
    boundary.

 -- Russell Wagner <152923636+SqueakyFloors@users.noreply.github.com>  26 Oct 2026 19:00:00 -0700

libbtbb (2020-12-R1+fork3) unstable; urgency=low
  * Adaptive frequency hopping (AFH) now follows the Core specification
    (Vol 2, Part B, 2.6.3.1): a basic-sequence channel the map marks used is
    kept, only an unused one is re-mapped, the peripheral slot repeats the
    central slot's channel, and a map with fewer than 20 used channels is not
    adapted to. Host-side hop predictions agreed with neither the
    specification nor the Ubertooth firmware before, so a clock and UAP
    tracked with AFH named channels the piconet was not on.
  * An adaptive piconet is no longer handed the hopping sequence cached for the
    same address and a different channel map.

 -- Russell Wagner <152923636+SqueakyFloors@users.noreply.github.com>  25 Oct 2026 19:45:00 -0700

libbtbb (2020-12-R1+fork2) unstable; urgency=low
  * LE decode complaints ("Error: attempt to read past end of buffer",
    "Early return due to 0 length" and the "Wrong length" messages) now go to
    stderr instead of into the packet dump on stdout. The messages themselves
    are unchanged.

